= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the two supports as $I_f=(l_f,r_f)$ and $I_g=(l_g,r_g)$. Every point in a support moves strictly right: a positive nonidentity <order automorphism> has no fixed point inside its one support interval. Choose $u\in I_f$, $v\in I_g$ and an increasing bijection $\theta:[u,uf]\to[v,vg]$ matching endpoints. The half-open intervals $[uf^n,uf^{n+1})$, for $n\in\mathbb Z$, partition $I_f$, because the iterates tend to its endpoints. Define
$$
(tf^n)b=(t\theta)g^n,\qquad u\le t<uf.
$$
The endpoint choices make adjacent pieces fit, giving an increasing bijection $b:I_f\to I_g$ with $fb=bg$. Extend over the two complementary closed rays by increasing maps taking $l_f,r_f$ to $l_g,r_g$. Outside the supports both automorphisms are identity, so $fb=bg$ still holds. Therefore $\boxed{b^{-1}fb=g}$. This gives the <conjugacy of positive real-line bumps>.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Construct the <conjugacy of positive real-line bumps> using $u=v=\alpha$. Since both $\alpha f$ and $\alpha g$ are greater than $\beta$, choose the fundamental-interval map to be identity on $[\alpha,\beta]$ and then increase from $\beta$ to the respective right endpoints. Equivariant extension along $f$ and $g$ gives a conjugator on their supports fixing $[\alpha,\beta]$. Extend it over the remaining portions of $J=[\gamma,\gamma h]$, fixing the endpoints of $J$; both support intervals lie strictly inside $J$. Call the resulting interval bijection $b_0$. It satisfies $fb_0=b_0g$ on $J$.

The point $\gamma$ lies in a positive orbital of $h$, because $\gamma h>\gamma$. Its half-open cells $J h^n$, $n\in\mathbb Z$, partition that orbital. Extend by
$$
(th^n)b=(tb_0)h^n,\qquad \gamma\le t<\gamma h,
$$
and set $b$ to identity outside the orbital. Since $b_0$ fixes the cell endpoints, this is an <order automorphism> and satisfies $bh=hb$. Each cell is preserved; $f$ and $g$ are identity on all cells other than the original one, so $fb=bg$ holds everywhere. Finally $b$ fixes $[\alpha,\beta]$ pointwise, and $a$ is supported inside that interval. Hence $ba=ab$. Thus
$$
\boxed{b^{-1}fb=g,\qquad ba=ab,\qquad bh=hb}.
$$
This is <conjugating positive bumps while preserving a fundamental interval>; merely choosing an arbitrary conjugator would not ensure either commutation relation.

= 5
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The additive <Abelian group> $G$ is divisible because it is a real <vector space>. Each convex subgroup $C_n$ is divisible too: for $c\ge0$ in $C_n$ and a positive integer $m$, its unique additive quotient $c/m$ satisfies $0\le c/m\le c$, hence belongs to $C_n$. Negative elements follow by taking inverses. Thus all $C_n$ are rational <vector subspaces>, independently of whether the given real scalar multiplication respects the order.

Choose an ordered-group isomorphism $\theta_n:C_{n+1}/C_n\to\mathbb R$. It is rational-linear, since additive homomorphisms between divisible torsion-free groups preserve division by integers. Extend a rational <basis> of $C_n$ to one of $C_{n+1}$; equivalently choose a rational-linear section $s_n:\mathbb R\to C_{n+1}$ of $\theta_n$ composed with the quotient map. Then
$$
C_{n+1}=C_n\oplus s_n(\mathbb R).
$$
Since $C_1=0$ and the union is $G$, each $g\in G$ has a unique expression $g=\sum_{j=1}^N s_j(r_j)$ with only finitely many nonzero coefficients. Define $\Phi(g)=(r_1,r_2,\ldots)$. This is an injective additive <group homomorphism>. If $r_N\ne0$ is the largest active coefficient, the image of $g$ in the ordered quotient $C_{N+1}/C_N$ has sign equal to that of $r_N$. Convexity ensures that this is also the sign of $g$.

The maximum-support convention for the <Hahn group> $V(\mathbb Z_{>0},\mathbb R)$ uses precisely finite supports and the sign of the largest active coefficient. Hence $\Phi$ preserves and reflects the <total order>, and therefore preserves <join> and <meet>. This proves the <finite-support Hahn embedding along a countable convex chain>. In fact every finite coefficient sequence is obtained, so $\Phi$ is an ordered-group isomorphism in this convention; the requested embedding follows.

