= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

**No: there are at most three, including zero and the whole group.** A two-generator ordered <Abelian group> is <torsion-free> and has <rank of an abelian group> at most two. A <convex subgroup of an ordered group> is pure: if $ng\in C$ with $n>0$, then $g$ lies between zero and $ng$ or their negatives, so $g\in C$. Therefore a proper inclusion $C<D$ of convex subgroups increases rational rank: an element of $D\setminus C$ cannot have a nonzero integer multiple in $C$. Convex subgroups form a chain, so a rank-two group permits at most two strict inclusions. This is the bound for <convex subgroups of a finite-rank ordered Abelian group>. It is sharp: <lexicographically ordered group> $\mathbb Z^2$ has exactly $0$, $\{0\}\times\mathbb Z$, and $\mathbb Z^2$.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A concrete model of the two-generated <free Abelian lattice-ordered group> is the lattice subgroup of real-valued functions on $\mathbb R^2$ generated by the coordinate projections $X(x,y)=x$, $Y(x,y)=y$. Its elements are finite <joins> of finite <meets> of integer linear forms $ax+by$. Thus they are continuous <positively homogeneous> <piecewise linear functions>, with pointwise addition and lattice operations. This is the <piecewise integer-linear representation of a free Abelian lattice-ordered group>.

For the construction, first form the term algebra on two symbols using addition, negation, zero, <join> and <meet>, then identify terms that agree in every <Abelian lattice-ordered group>. Evaluation under any assignment of the two generators factors through this quotient, proving its <universal property>. To obtain the concrete function model, use the identity-detection theorem that the real line generates the variety of Abelian lattice-ordered groups. Its proof passes a failed identity to a prime ordered quotient, chooses its finitely many active linear pieces, and uses the rational-inequality alternative underlying <Farkas' lemma> to find a real witness. This makes evaluation on $\mathbb R^2$ injective as well as surjective onto the generated function group.

**Its underlying group is not $\mathbb Z^n$ for any finite $n$.** The <integer-linear hinge functions have infinite independent rank>: $h_k(x,y)=(x-ky)\vee0$ are integer-linearly independent for distinct integers $k$. On $y=1$, only $h_k$ has a slope jump at $x=k$, so each coefficient in a finite relation must vanish. The underlying group therefore has infinite <rank of an abelian group> despite having two lattice generators.

= iii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No.** The <free Abelian lattice-ordered group> on two generators is <countable>, since every element is represented by a finite term over a countable alphabet of operation symbols and the two generators. The additive <group> of all real-valued functions on the given half-plane is uncountable: it already contains a distinct constant function for every <real number>. A <lattice-ordered group homomorphism> that is an isomorphism would be a bijection, contradicting these cardinalities. The concrete free-group model consists of specially constrained integer-linear term functions, not all real-valued functions on a domain.

= iv
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**No.** Assign the two free generators to $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ in the <lexicographically ordered group> $\mathbb Z^2$. The <universal property> gives a <lattice-ordered group homomorphism> onto this group: its image contains both ordinary group generators. It is not an <Archimedean ordered group>, since
$$
n(0,1)<(1,0)\qquad\text{for every positive integer }n.
$$
Thus a lattice-homomorphic image can contain a nonzero positive infinitesimal even when the original free object has an Archimedean pointwise function representation.
